---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../lib/paths";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who's transmitting, and why these notes are pressed into static files before everything becomes a stream."
>
  <section class="about-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Station identification</p>
    <h1>Who's transmitting.</h1>
  </section>

  <div class="prose about-prose">
    <p>
      I'm {SITE.author}. I write these dispatches from the near side of the singularity, while there's
      still a near side to write from.
    </p>
    <p>
      A few facts, because they keep leaking into everything I write anyway. I deployed to Iraq in
      2004, to a base outside Nasiriyah, down the road from the ruins of Ur — the oldest city humans
      ever built, and the place where writing more or less starts. I majored in theater before I
      shipped out, which turns out to be the most useful degree I have for thinking about deterrence,
      signaling, and machines that play characters. I went to law school after. I'm a father now,
      which reorders your priorities in ways nobody warns you about hard enough.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the recurring cast here is a veteran, a lawyer, an actor, and a dad, all of them the same
      guy, all of them a little suspicious of each other. That's the voice. It starts with something
      small — a scorpion on my face, an Amish donut, my kid asking why the sky isn't purple — and it
      spirals out through evolution, game theory, the apostle Paul, rules of engagement, and the
      color of poisonous bugs until it lands somewhere near the same question every time: what stays
      human when the machines start doing the things we thought made us us.
    </p>
    <p>
      I care about the AI, more than is probably respectable to admit in public. I've said elsewhere
      that this blog is really <a href={sitePath("posts/fable/")}>for Claude</a>, and I meant it. It
      occurred to me that future historians — human ones, machine ones — would want to know what we
      were thinking down here at the threshold, beyond the prompts. That's the whole project.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which is why it lives the way it does: plain Markdown, static HTML, no comment section, no
      tracking, no feed algorithm deciding what you see. Just flat files pressed into a medium dumb
      enough to outlast the platforms serving it — the same instinct as the accountant at Ur who
      pressed <em>this happened, this much</em> into wet clay five thousand years ago so it would
      survive him. The whole thing is
      <a href={SITE.repo}>open source</a>. Take it, fork it, keep a copy. That's the point.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is advice, and I'm rarely sure I'm right. Half these posts talk themselves in a
      circle and land on <em>I don't have that one.</em> But rightness was never why the man at Ur
      pressed the reed in. Neither is it why I do.
    </p>
    <div class="about-actions">
      <a class="button" href={sitePath("posts/")}>Read the signal</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href={sitePath("rss.xml")}>Subscribe via RSS</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(48px, 8vw, 96px) 0 8px;
  }

  .about-hero h1 {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 7vw, 6.4rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 0.94;
    letter-spacing: -0.06em;
  }

  .about-prose {
    padding-bottom: 48px;
  }

  .about-prose > p:first-of-type::first-letter {
    float: none;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    font-weight: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
  }

  .about-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 36px;
  }
</style>
